    "chunk_text": "Sir, now, I will give a brief overview of the rescue, rehabilitation and insolvency legal regime. All economies in the world, both corporate and personal, function on credit. Therefore, efficient management of credit and recoveries are essential to the smooth functioning of an economy. In every economy, there would be situations where people or business entities would be in financial distress or facing debt default. Some of them will result in corporate failures and personal insolvency situations. Therefore, without robust laws to deal with such situations, business and personal financial failures could create chaos in a society. We saw many situations like that in the past, perhaps, due to Cyclone \"Ditwah\" or\n\nAs far as the shortcomings of the current legislation are concerned, liquidation functions on insolvency. That is, failure of liquidity, which is the inability to pay debts, as they become due. This prevents further credit, even if there is a possibility or potential for a business to be rescued or resurrected. The result is, even potentially viable businesses often end up being liquidated. The consequences are, businesses in distress often delay action until collapse, enter into informal workouts often lacking transparency to stakeholders, banks accumulate non-performing loans and economic value is often destroyed rather than preserved."
  },
