    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "48a332e9393b690921d621671de5423a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1985,
    "chunk_text": "As far as the shortcomings of the current legislation are concerned, liquidation functions on insolvency. That is, failure of liquidity, which is the inability to pay debts, as they become due. This prevents further credit, even if there is a possibility or potential for a business to be rescued or resurrected. The result is, even potentially viable businesses often end up being liquidated. The consequences are, businesses in distress often delay action until collapse, enter into informal workouts often lacking transparency to stakeholders, banks accumulate non-performing loans and economic value is often destroyed rather than preserved.\n\n1071 1072\n\n\u0db4\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dbd\u0dd2\u0ddc\u0db8\u0dca\u0db1\u0dca\u0dad\u0dd4\u0db8\u0dc0\n\nas updated and supplemented, the Insolvency, Restructuring and Dissolution Act of 2018 of Singapore and the Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code of 2016 of India are some others.\n\nBusiness continuity and employment preservation are other important aspects which enable a business to be sold \u201cas a whole\u201d without dismantling assets and businesses to recover.\n\nNow, I will cite a few well-known examples of restructuring under Chapter 11 of the USA. The restructuring of the Delta Airlines was commenced in 2005 and its revival was made in 2007. Then, the United Airlines' revival was in 2006 and the General Motors' revival took place within 40 days after commencing its restructuring in 2009! - [Interruption.] The restructuring of Marvel Entertainment was commenced in 1996 and the revival was made in 1998.\n\nNow, if we take the reform efforts in Sri Lanka, there has been consistent calls for reforms by business owners and Chambers of Commerce.\n\nIt has always been contemplated that a comprehensive legal regime for rescue, rehabilitation and insolvency would be introduced in Sri Lanka. Therefore, even when the Companies Act was being reformed, the insolvency regime was not substantially reformed because even at the\n\ntime, the discussion was to bring in this Bill separately."
  },
