    "chunk_size_bytes": 2145,
    "chunk_text": "\u0d9c\u0dbb\u0dd4 \u0db1\u0dd3\u0dad\u0dd2\u0dc3\u0da5 \u0dbb\u0dd4\u0dbb\u0dc6\u0dca \u0dc3\u0dd3\u0db8\u0dca \u0db8\u0dc3\u200d\u0dcf (\u0bae\u0bbe\u0ba3\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bc1\u0bae\u0bbf\u0b95\u0bc1 \u0b9a\u0b9f\u0bcd\u0b9f\u0ba4\u0bcd\u0ba4\u0bb0\u0ba3\u0bbf \u0bb0\u0b8a\u0baa\u0bcd \u0bb9\u0b95\u0bc0\u0bae\u0bcd) (The Hon. Rauff Hakeem, Attorney-at-Law) Hon. Presiding Member, I saw that the Bank Supervision Department of the Central Banks has said that only about Rs. 2,000 million had been disbursed by the banks in the Kandy District, whereas Rs. 4,000 million in Badulla District and Rs. 3,000 million in Nuwara Eliya District had been disbursed. But, in the most affected district, the Kandy District, only Rs. 2,000 million had been disbursed. That is because of the reluctance and the risk-averse attitude of the banks and that has to be addressed. I have been looking forward - [Interruption.]- Not only that. - [Interruption.] I know. Now, let me finish because I have limited time.\n\nThank you. \u0d9c\u0dbb\u0dd4 \u0db8\u0dd6\u0dbd\u0dcf\u0dc3\u0db1\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0dd6\u0daa \u0db8\u0db1\u0dca\u0dd3\u0dad\u0dd4\u0db8\u0db8\u0dcf (\u0bae\u0bbe\u0ba3\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bc1\u0bae\u0bbf\u0b95\u0bc1 \u0ba4\u0b95\u0bb2\u0b95\u0bae\u0ba4\u0bbe\u0b99\u0bcd\u0b95\u0bc1\u0bae\u0bcd \u0b89\u0bb1\u0bc1\u0baa\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bbf\u0ba9\u0bb0\u0bcd \u0b85\u0bb5\u0bb0\u0bcd\u0b95\u0bb3\u0bcd) (The Hon. Presiding Member) \u0d9c\u0dbb\u0dd4 \u0dbb\u0dd3 \u0d9a\u0dbb\u0dd4\u0dab\u0dcf\u0db1\u0dcf\u0dba\u0d9a \u0db8\u0db1\u0dca\u0dd3\u0dad\u0dd4\u0db8\u0db8\u0dcf. \u0d94\u0db6\u0dad\u0dd4\u0db8\u0db8\u0dcf \u0dc0\u0dd2\u0db1\u0dcf\u0da9\u0dd2 \u0dc3\u200d\u0d9a \u0d9a\u0dcf\u0dbd\u0dba\u0d9a\u0dca \u0dad\u0dd2\u0dc3\u0ddc\u0db6\u0db1\u0dc0\u0dcf.\n\n[4.56 p.m.] \u0d9c\u0dbb\u0dd4 \u0dbb\u0dd3 \u0d9a\u0dbb\u0dd4\u0dab\u0dcf\u0db1\u0dcf\u0dba\u0d9a \u0db8\u0dc3\u200d\u0dcf (\u0bae\u0bbe\u0ba3\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bc1\u0bae\u0bbf\u0b95\u0bc1 \u0bb0\u0bb5\u0bbf \u0b95\u0bb0\u0bc1\u0ba3\u0bbe\u0ba8\u0bbe\u0bc8\u0b95\u0bcd\u0b95) (The Hon. Ravi Karunanayake) Thank you, Mr. Presiding Member, for giving me the opportunity to speak on this Bill.\n\nWhile stating that the introduction of this Rescue, Rehabilitation and Insolvency (Corporate and Personal) Bill is timely, I must also tell you that it something that has been brought into existence through commissioning by Mr. Chanaka de Silva, President's Counsel, in June, 2023.\n\nSir, it needs many corrections.\n\nI spoke to the Hon. Minister of Justice and National Integration today and he said that owing to the time factor, they would like to see this legislated and that\n\namendments could be brought in later. But, I thought I would contribute in that regard during the little time available to me to make it more meaningful."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
    "doc_id": "2026-05-06-2026-05-06",
    "num": "2026-05-06",
    "date_str": "2026-05-06",
    "description": "Hansard of 2026-05-06",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/23536_english_2026-05-06.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-05-06-2026-05-06-0225",
    "chunk_index": 225,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "a7e28e39fa856e78133bb26a203349f3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1806,
    "chunk_text": "I spoke to the Hon. Minister of Justice and National Integration today and he said that owing to the time factor, they would like to see this legislated and that\n\namendments could be brought in later. But, I thought I would contribute in that regard during the little time available to me to make it more meaningful.\n\nWhile appreciating the legislation of this Act, the rectifications that need to be done to it are as follows. My feeling is that the 60-day time frame has got to be looked at since it is hardly sufficient because the decision-making process in Sri Lanka is not as competent as it should be. So, my view is that we should look at a 180-day period because it would be routed through the courts with a windup officer being appointed. So, my feeling is that we should introduce a realistic 180-day window at the initial stage.\n\nThen, we would also have to look at prioritization of small and medium enterprises - SMEs. Small and medium enterprises, I would say, are the most important aspect of our GDP. Almost 60 to 65 per cent of the companies in our country are small and medium enterprises, and they are the ones which are severely affected by this. Therefore, there should be a process for prioritization of SMEs, and that process should go on.\n\nI see the Hon. Minister of Justice and National Integration walking in and I only want to reiterate the matters I referred to earlier. The process would be a court process. So, I would say that specific district courts need to be established for this particular purpose like the commercial courts that had been established to fast-track the process.\n\nNow, let us take the negative aspects pointed out by those who came before the Committee on Public Finance. One thing that had been pointed out is that this system should operate islandwide."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
    "doc_id": "2026-05-06-2026-05-06",
    "num": "2026-05-06",
    "date_str": "2026-05-06",
    "description": "Hansard of 2026-05-06",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/23536_english_2026-05-06.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-05-06-2026-05-06-0226",
    "chunk_index": 226,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "a8ee9aa34663ea91ed515cfbd0118070",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1654,
    "chunk_text": "I see the Hon. Minister of Justice and National Integration walking in and I only want to reiterate the matters I referred to earlier. The process would be a court process. So, I would say that specific district courts need to be established for this particular purpose like the commercial courts that had been established to fast-track the process.\n\nNow, let us take the negative aspects pointed out by those who came before the Committee on Public Finance. One thing that had been pointed out is that this system should operate islandwide.\n\nIt goes unsaid that the temporary suspension of the parate law is important. I say that the parate law is something which has to be addressed separately. The parate law relates to the insolvency aspect of it and I will deal with it after I finish speaking about the Rescue, Rehabilitation and Insolvency (Corporate and Personal) Bill that we hope to pass.\n\n1181 1182\n\n2026 \u0db8\u0dd0\u0dba\u0dd2 06\n\nThen, when we look at cross-border insolvency, it necessitates that we look at the foreign exchange aspect of it. The Foreign Exchange Department of the Central Bank will have to look at the impact of internationalizing local debt. If you have a company that runs aground and they syndicate it - you might have many of those companies amalgamated - and take it overseas, making the insolvency cross-border, then you would have foreign domination. When foreign domination comes in, there would be dollar perspective to be looked at. Then, we will have to consider the country\u2019s risk aspect in that. Also, the Central Bank's accepting dollar loans for rupee loans would be a matter that we would have to consider further."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
    "doc_id": "2026-05-06-2026-05-06",
    "num": "2026-05-06",
    "date_str": "2026-05-06",
    "description": "Hansard of 2026-05-06",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/23536_english_2026-05-06.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-05-06-2026-05-06-0227",
    "chunk_index": 227,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "cd61dc2243877098622450403d263e65",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1980,
    "chunk_text": "Then, when we look at cross-border insolvency, it necessitates that we look at the foreign exchange aspect of it. The Foreign Exchange Department of the Central Bank will have to look at the impact of internationalizing local debt. If you have a company that runs aground and they syndicate it - you might have many of those companies amalgamated - and take it overseas, making the insolvency cross-border, then you would have foreign domination. When foreign domination comes in, there would be dollar perspective to be looked at. Then, we will have to consider the country\u2019s risk aspect in that. Also, the Central Bank's accepting dollar loans for rupee loans would be a matter that we would have to consider further.\n\nThen, you look at taxation. That has been dealt with in the Insolvency Act rather than being looked at from the taxation laws' point of view. That was mentioned by Mr. Suresh Perera of KPMG, who is preeminent in that area, pointing out that there is a necessity to incorporate it in tax amendments. So, I guess it has got to be looked at from that perspective because, otherwise, the Inland Revenue Department might say, \u201cSorry, it does not affect us\u201d. So, on that basis, this Act, by the time it comes into effect, would lose its value.\n\nThe next matter is the fear of the banks owing to the parate law. Not only that, owing to the fact that the NDB is now going through some financial crisis, you might have a situation where even a bank would seek redress through this law. So, we must look at it from a practical aspect and how to improve from a business sense. Insolvency happens due to bad policies, bad business decisions or high interest rates such the increase on 12th April, 2022 to 70 per cent while companies are not prepared to accept external and internal shocks.. So, on that score, we have got to help the small and medium enterprises and the big businesses which have a good business propositions but not a sufficient capital component."
  },
