    "md5": "1dc0cbbfe6238983ce4a7ba9906ef40d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1641,
    "chunk_text": "However, today, my focus is on Clauses 31(4) and 34. Clause 31(4) proposes new Sections 163(4A) to (4H). I believe the Government is going to amend it. I do not know whether that amendment is coming in. Clause 31(4) empowers the Commissioner-General to issue a certificate of default to the Magistrate\u2019s Court, wherein the tax in default is considered a fine for a criminal offence. Furthermore, Section 163(4C) even bars the Magistrate from questioning the correctness of the certificate. There, the power of the Judiciary is taken out and that is not separation of powers. Further, under Section 163(4H), even if an appeal is pending, a certificate is treated as conclusive evidence. So, that is a draconian piece of legislation. The Court held that that is arbitrary, irrational and contrary to Article 12(1) of the Constitution. Therefore, they said it has to be amended, if it is going to be passed by simple majority. I believe the Government would amend it; I hope the Government would actually look at amending this Clause.\n\nWith regard to conviction with fines and imprisonment, the Court rejected the challenge to Clause 34, which criminalize compliance failure. Compliance failure is a very scary thing. The Court only looks at constitutional inconsistency and also at legal infringement because they look through a narrow window. That is, the court\u2019s issue. But, policymakers - the parliamentarians and the Government - have to look beyond it. When you bring in a new law, you have to look at what its economic impact, the social impact, the democratic impact and the impacts on the people are and whether it harasses people."
  },
  {
