    "chunk_size_bytes": 2160,
    "chunk_text": "\u0d9c\u0dd4 (\u0dc0\u0dc0\u0daf\u0dba) \u0dbb\u0dd2\u0dc3\u0db1\u0dc0\u0dd2 \u0dc3\u0dcf\u0dd2\u0d9b \u0db8\u0dad\u0dad\u0dcf (\u0db1\u0dd2\u0dc0\u0dba\u0ddd\u0da2\u0dba \u0d9a\u0dae\u0dcf\u0db1\u0dcf\u0dba\u0d9a\u0dd4\u0dbb\u0db8\u0dcf \u0dc3\u0dad \u0d9a\u0dcf\u0dad\u0d9a \u0dc3\u0db7\u0dcf \u0dc3\u0db7\u0dcf\u0db4\u0dad\u0dd2\u0dd4\u0dbb\u0db8\u0dcf) (\u0bc7\u0bc6\u0bbe\u0ba3\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bc1\u0bc7\u0bbf\u0b95\u0bc1 (\u0bae\u0bb5\u0ba4\u0bcd\u0ba4\u0bbf\u0baf\u0bc6\u0bbe\u0bcd) \u0bc6\u0bbe\u0bbf\u0bb8\u0bcd\u0bb5\u0bbf \u0b9a\u0bc6\u0bbe\u0bb2\u0bbf - \u0baa\u0bbf\u0bb0\u0ba4\u0bbf\u0b9a\u0bcd \u0b9a\u0baa\u0bc6\u0bbe\u0ba8\u0bc6\u0bbe\u0baf\u0b95\u0bb0\u0bc1\u0bae\u0bcd \u0b95\u0bc1\u0bb4\u0bc1\u0b95\u0bcd\u0b95\u0bb3\u0bbf\u0ba9\u0bcd \u0ba4\u0bb5\u0bbf\u0b9a\u0bc6\u0bbe\u0bb3\u0bb0\u0bc1\u0bae\u0bcd) (The Hon. (Dr.) Rizvie Salih - Deputy Speaker and Chair of Committees) Hon. Speaker, I rise today to speak on this Adjournment Debate on the Annual Economic Review, 2025 of the Central Bank not only with a sense of responsibility, but also with a convinced sense of hope.\n\nSir, we all remember the dark days our country went through; those memories are still fresh in the minds of our people. We remember the long fuel queues; we remember parents wondering how they would feed their children; we remember pharmacies running short of medicines; we remember the power cuts, the uncertainty and the fear, frustration and anger of the people. I am not here to play the blame game, but to pay tribute to the ordinary citizens of this country for bearing all that during its most difficult period. The farmer continued to cultivate despite rising costs, the fisherman still went out to sea, the teacher continued educating children despite hardships and the doctor, the nurse, the public servant, the labourer and the small businessman, all of them kept this nation functioning when the situation seemed almost impossible. Therefore, before speaking about growth rates and reserves, I believe this House must first pay tribute to the resilience of the people of Sri Lanka.\n\nSir, against this difficult background, the economic performance in 2025 truly deserves recognition and applause. Most importantly, it means that confidence is slowly returning in this country and that the economy is on the way to recovery. And, confidence is one of the most important and valuable assets this nation could possess. When people lose confidence in their economy, they stop investing, producing and hoping. But when confidence returns, recovery begins."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
    "doc_id": "2026-05-20-2026-05-20",
    "num": "2026-05-20",
    "date_str": "2026-05-20",
    "description": "Hansard of 2026-05-20",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/23618_english_2026-05-20.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-05-20-2026-05-20-0083",
    "chunk_index": 83,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "312ccf759255d54d4ce91fdc216dc3c8",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2007,
    "chunk_text": "Sir, against this difficult background, the economic performance in 2025 truly deserves recognition and applause. Most importantly, it means that confidence is slowly returning in this country and that the economy is on the way to recovery. And, confidence is one of the most important and valuable assets this nation could possess. When people lose confidence in their economy, they stop investing, producing and hoping. But when confidence returns, recovery begins.\n\n1849 1850\n\n2026 \u0db8\u0dd0\u0dba\u0dd2 20\n\nsmall businesses survive without fear? The positive answers we see today are what deserve applause and what matter most.\n\nSir, I must also mention the remarkable contribution made by Sri Lankans working overseas. Once again, worker remittances reached historically high levels and became a major strength for our economy. Many of our brothers and sisters work abroad under difficult conditions. They miss weddings, funerals, birthdays and the precious years of their children growing up. Some work day and night in unfamiliar lands simply to support families back in Sri Lanka. This sacrifice deserves the gratitude of this nation and it is they who display true patriotism. In many ways, our migrant workers have become silent economic heroes of Sri Lanka.\n\nSir, another positive development has been the strengthening of the external sector. Despite global uncertainty and the widening trade deficit, Sri Lanka continued to maintain a Current Account surplus. The Review also highlights improvements in the financial sector. Banks and financial institutions strengthened their profitability and asset quality. Liquidity and capital buffers remained above regulatory requirements.\n\nSir, a stable banking system means that depositors feel secure, businesses could obtain credit, entrepreneurs could expand operations and families could invest in homes, education and livelihoods. The expansion of private sector credit during 2025 reflects the gradual revival of economic activity and business confidence."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
    "doc_id": "2026-05-20-2026-05-20",
    "num": "2026-05-20",
    "date_str": "2026-05-20",
    "description": "Hansard of 2026-05-20",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/23618_english_2026-05-20.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-05-20-2026-05-20-0084",
    "chunk_index": 84,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "2a92fdd7070a232b7f92f6b027c8feed",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1717,
    "chunk_text": "Sir, a stable banking system means that depositors feel secure, businesses could obtain credit, entrepreneurs could expand operations and families could invest in homes, education and livelihoods. The expansion of private sector credit during 2025 reflects the gradual revival of economic activity and business confidence.\n\nHowever, Sir, while there is reason for optimism, there is absolutely no room and there shall not be room for complacency. The global environment remains extremely uncertain. The erratic, continued conflict in the Middle East poses risks to energy prices, global trade, tourism and financial flows. Also, climate-related disasters are becoming more frequent and more destructive. Sri Lanka is highly vulnerable to such shocks.\n\nA rise in global oil prices affects transport, electricity, food prices and ultimately, the daily lives of our people. A climate disaster could destroy crops, livelihoods and entire communities within days. Therefore, we must continue to build economic resilience while maintaining fiscal discipline and policy consistency and that, we will certainly do. The mistakes in the past will never be repeated. One important lesson we must learn is that sustainable recovery cannot be built on short-term political popularity alone. Sound economic management, sometimes, requires difficult decisions. But, those decisions will always be accompanied by compassion and\n\nsocial protection. Economic reform without humanity creates resentment. At the same time, unlimited populism giveaways without responsibility destroy nations. As a popular Government, we will balance both responsibility and compassion and that balance is important when supporting vulnerable communities."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
    "doc_id": "2026-05-20-2026-05-20",
    "num": "2026-05-20",
    "date_str": "2026-05-20",
    "description": "Hansard of 2026-05-20",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/23618_english_2026-05-20.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-05-20-2026-05-20-0085",
    "chunk_index": 85,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "536ad28d3e080ccfc9d5f200bf8aa124",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1870,
    "chunk_text": "social protection. Economic reform without humanity creates resentment. At the same time, unlimited populism giveaways without responsibility destroy nations. As a popular Government, we will balance both responsibility and compassion and that balance is important when supporting vulnerable communities.\n\nSir, I particularly wish to speak about our youth. Our young people need more than speeches; they need opportunities, skills, innovation and confidence. Confidence could build successful lives within Sri Lanka itself. If our youth lose faith in this country, no economic statistic could save us. Therefore, development must be people-centred, future-oriented and inclusive. That precisely is the intention and the path of this Government.\n\nSir, Sri Lanka has suffered greatly in recent years. But, this nation has also shown extraordinary resilience. And today, despite all the challenges before us, Sri Lanka is regaining stability, rebuilding confidence and restoring hope. This progress did not happen accidentally; it came through sacrifice, discipline and reforms during difficult times and above all, through the patience and endurance of the people of Sri Lanka.\n\nSir, therefore, let us, together, move forward with humility, wisdom and responsibility to strengthen institutions instead of weakening them, to encourage production instead of dependency, to create opportunities instead of divisions and to build a Sri Lanka where economic stability is matched by social justice and national unity.\n\nLet us never forget that the true strength of a country is not measured only by GDP growth or foreign reserves. The true strength of a country lies in the dignity of its people, the hope of its youth, the trust of its institutions and the belief that tomorrow could be better than today. Sri Lanka has endured hardship, faced uncertainty, but is rising again."
  },
