    "chunk_text": "Hon. Deputy Speaker, you also dealt with that in your speech. And, it is after a long time that we heard you speak. Once in a way, the Members presiding must take their chance and share their ideas with us. It is very good.\n\nSir, the talking point today is the inordinate depreciation of the rupee against the dollar. Yesterday's \"Daily Mirror\" also had a headline, which I referred to\n\n2026 \u0db8\u0dd0\u0dba\u0dd2 20\n\nand on which there was a big commotion in the House. The Leader of the House did not want me to go on. When I was reading it as the Pro-tem - the temporary - Chair of the Committee on Public Finance, I pointed out the issue relating to the Central Bank\u2019s difficulty in managing the dollar fluctuation. Yesterday\u2019s headline news is repeated again today by that newspaper in a special article captioned \u201cRupee depreciation\u2019s double edge\u201d by Dr. Kenneth De Zilwa, Global Business Cycle Economist. There, he clearly states one thing, which I think is very important for us to record here. On the need to have a careful way forward, he says, I quote:\n\n\u201cThe Central Bank faces a delicate balancing act. Defending the anchor requires reserves, but those are low and IMF targets prevent aggressive selling.\u201d\n\nThe IMF conditions require that you cannot intervene in the foreign exchange market. For the first time, we saw the Central Bank purchasing about 10 to 12 million dollar reserves from the market. That was after a very long time. But, they have now given it up. So, there cannot be such intervention. They also cannot print money, which is also bound by the new Central Bank of Sri Lanka Act. We have given them independence. The Governor had made a very clear position regarding all this.\n\nThe article further states, I quote:\n\n\u201cAllowing depreciation without intervention expands money supply and credit, fueling inflation and thus warranting a tightening cycle for shocks caused by depreciation due to external imbalances and lack of USD cashflows, -\u201d"
