    "chunk_text": "\u0db8\u0db1\u0dca \u0d85\u0db1\u0dd4\u0dbb \u0d9a\u0db1\u0dca \u0dcf\u0dd2\u0db1\u0dbd\u0d9a \u0db8\u0dc4\u0dc3\u0dad\u0dcf \u0dc1\u0dc0\u0dbb\u0dcf\u0dba \u0dca\u0dc4\u0dc3 \u0dc3\u0dd2\u0dc0\u0dd2\u0dbd\u0dca \u0d9c\u0dd4\u0dc0\u0dca \u0dc0\u0dca\u0db4\u0dc0\u0dcf \u0d85\u0db8\u0dcf\u0dad\u0dba \u0dca\u0dc4\u0dc3 \u0db6\u0dbd\u0d9b\u0dca\u0dd2\u0db1 \u0d85\u0db8\u0dcf\u0dad\u0dba\u0dad\u0dd4 \u0db8\u0dcf (\u0bae\u0bbe\u0ba3\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bc1\u0bae\u0bbf\u0b95\u0bc1 \u0b85\u0ba9\u0bc1\u0bb0 \u0b95\u0bb0\u0bc1\u0bc8\u0bbe\u0ba4\u0bbf\u0bb2\u0b95\u0bcd\u0b95 - \u0ba4\u0bc1\u0b95\u0bb1\u0bae\u0bc1\u0b95\u0b99\u0bcd\u0b95\u0bb3\u0bcd \u0bae\u0bb1\u0bcd\u0bb1\u0bc1\u0bae\u0bcd \u0b9a\u0bbf\u0bb5\u0bbf\u0bb2\u0bcd \u0bb5\u0bbf\u0bae\u0bbe\u0bc8 \u0b9a\u0b9a\u0b95\u0bb5\u0b95\u0bb3\u0bcd \u0b85\u0b95\u0bae\u0b9a\u0bcd\u0b9a\u0bb0\u0bcd) (The Hon. Anura Karunathilaka - Minister of Ports and Civil Aviation and Minister of Energy) Hon. Speaker, the Answer to the Questions raised by Hon. Ravi Karunanayake is as follows:\n\n1. Total outstanding payments presently due to\n\nThe breakdown of the average cost per unit is as follows:\n\n2. Payments to thermal power suppliers have been\n\nprioritized is a wrong interpretation. We have not prioritized the thermal power supply. Our priority is always to maintain stability of the powe supply. The National System Operator\u2019s priority has been to ensure a continuous electricity supply despite the record-high electricity demand, increased fuel prices and low hydro conditions. So, no priority has been given to thermal power plants, as the question states. Priority has been given to ensure a continuous supply of fuel to power plants. So, the cash flow was managed to achieve that objective.\n\nEnergy Source Average cost per\n\nunit (Rs.)\n\nMajor hydro 2.05 Mini hydro 14.67 Solar (Rooftop and ground-mounted solar)\n\n25.13\n\nWind 17.40 Biomass 38.70\n\nWaste to energy 36.20\n\nThe average unit cost for thermal power generation projected for 2026 from interim tariff filing is, Rs. 31.99.\n\n3. Only energy costs are provided and capacity costs\n\nhave been excluded.\n\n4. Again, what is given in the question is a wrong\n\ninterpretation. There is no policy basis for continuously curtailing renewable energy generation. Any curtailment has been purely due to technical reasons. The system stability is at risk when there is a low demand and a high renewable energy generation, especially during weekends and holidays."
