    "chunk_text": "interpretation. There is no policy basis for continuously curtailing renewable energy generation. Any curtailment has been purely due to technical reasons. The system stability is at risk when there is a low demand and a high renewable energy generation, especially during weekends and holidays.\n\nSo, with these statistics, we can clearly see that the Government has focused on increasing the renewable energy supply. At the same time, I would like to mention that up to 2024, we had only a percentage of 6.2 of rooftop solar. However, by 2025, it had been increased to 10.7 per cent and as of April, 2026, it is 13.3 per cent. So, you can clearly see that rooftop solar has doubled during the past one-and-a-half years. And, that clearly indicates that, as a Government, we have given priority to increase the renewable energy component of our power generation.\n\n5. What is mentioned in the question is, again, based on\n\nwrong information. There is no evidence that BESS tariff and incentive structures have been unattractive because the recent tender attracted 153 bids from 38 bidders and very competitive prices were achieved. So, that means there is no question of BESS being attractive.\n\n6. The total number of renewable energy developers\n\naffected by delayed payments is, 386. The other information is not available at the moment. Once I get it, I will let you know, Hon. Member.\n\n7. The NSO made a payment of Rs. 2 billion on 19th\n\nMay, 2026, a few days ago. Another Rs. 1 billion will be paid to renewable energy plants next week. So, we are optimistic to fully settle the outstanding balance before the end of June.\n\nYear Thermal Renewables (excl. EGL Hydro)\n\n2024 29.86 19.98 2025 28.11 21.92 2026 (from tariff filing)\n\n31.99 23.38\n\n2033 2034\n\n[\u0db8\u0db1\u0dca \u0dbb\u0dd3 \u0d9a\u0db1\u0dca \u0dcf\u0db1\u0dcf\u0dba\u0d9a \u0db8\u0dc4\u0dc3\u0dad\u0dcf]\n\n2026 \u0db8\u0dd0\u0dba\u0dd2 21\n\n8. The Cabinet has approved the National Electri