    "chunk_size_bytes": 2012,
    "chunk_text": "A widely accepted principle in many countries is that individuals engaged in political struggles or protests should not be treated as ordinary criminals, but as political prisoners.\n\nIn line with internationally recognized standards, including those of the Council of Europe, a \"political prisoner\" may be understood as a person whose detention\n\n2035 2036\n\n\u0db4\u0dcf\u0dca\u0dbd\u0dd2\u0db8\u0dc0\u0db1\u0dca\u0dad\u0dd4 \u0dc0\n\nSri Lanka itself provides a clear precedent. Following past insurrections, individuals associated with the Janatha Vimukthi Peramuna - the JVP - were granted amnesty, released and reintegrated into democratic political life with many, today, holding positions of public office. Sir, most Members across the aisle, if pardon had not been given, cannot even hold those positions today.\n\nThis demonstrates that political conflicts have previously been addressed through political and restorative measures rather than prolonged punitive detention.\n\nHowever, a disparity appears to exist in the continued detention and legal treatment of individuals connected to the Tamil political struggle, many of whom have been held for prolonged periods, often under the Prevention of Terrorism Act, PTA, which this Government also promised to repeal and which they have not done to date, with significant delays in indictments and trials. This raises serious concerns regarding consistency, proportionality and equality before the law.\n\nSir, there are some individuals who have been in prisons for 15, 20 or 30 years. I have the whole list of names and the number of years they have been detained.\n\nIt is also pertinent that the National People\u2019s Power - the NPP - pledged in its election Manifesto to address the issue of political prisoners.\n\nSir, in light of the above, I raise the following questions:\n\n1. Will the Ministry of Justice and National Integration provide a list of individuals who have been in prolonged detention, including those held under the PTA, together with the current status of proceedings in each case?"
